{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## 2600 patchset 2: the fix for the repo-wide CI failure would itself have broken everyone's CI\n\nThe Verified -1 on 2600 patchset 1 was OURS, not the pre-existing flake, and the substance is worse\nthan the message. test_predates_gate_guard_parity flagged an unguarded 'make e2e-deps' step. Because\nthe Verified lane runs workflows from TRUSTED MAIN against an arbitrary patchset, a base predating\nthis change has no e2e-deps rule, so the bare invocation fails the step -- the fix for a repo-wide CI\nfailure would have cast red votes on every other open change. Now guarded with a 'make -n e2e-deps'\nprobe, which also keeps the guard reading the tree's own Makefile, the only thing that can answer the\nquestion.\n\nLOCAL/CI SKEW THAT HID IT, worth generalizing: the gate considers only GIT-TRACKED paths. A local run\nof 16718 tests was green while tests/e2e/_toolchain.py was still untracked, and CI failed the moment\nit was committed. Re-running that gate after 'git add' is the habit that catches the class.\n\nBLOCKING FINDING CLOSED with four tests driving the REAL conftest through a REAL nested pytest run\n(pytester), over a replica of the e2e tree with stub node/npm on PATH. The load-bearing one is\n'--collect-only provisions': no test runs, so a fixture-only implementation would leave nothing\ninstalled -- that assertion is what separates the hook from the fallback. Eight mutations applied,\nall eight killed.\n\nA VACUOUS TEST WAS FOUND AND THE MECHANISM DELETED RATHER THAN DEFENDED. Removing\n'if _PROVISION_ATTEMPTED: return' changed nothing observable, because pytest_collection_modifyitems\nfires once per session -- it was dead mechanism. Deleting the flag was the right call over writing a\ntest to protect it. That is the correct response to a mutation that cannot be killed, and it is worth\nnaming as a positive pattern: an unkillable mutation means the code is dead, not that the test is\nmissing.\n\nCORRECTION TO MY OWN ESCALATION. I relayed playwright as 'dominating the cold install'. True in BYTES\nbut not for the reason implied: the published playwright package has NO postinstall script, so\nnothing downloads browsers on this path. Measured: full npm ci = 15 packages / 30M; --omit=optional =\n11 / 12M; playwright + playwright-core = 16.8M = 56% of the tree. Real lever, taken (now an\noptionalDependency), but the wrong-cause framing was kept out of the commit message.\n\nALSO NOT OBTAINABLE FROM THIS HOST, recorded so nobody retries it: wall-clock comparison of the\nplaywright lever. Paired cold runs gave --omit=optional 111.8s, full 63.4s, then --omit=optional\n420.9s. Registry variance dominates and INVERTS the result. Counts and bytes are the only stable\nevidence available locally.\n\nSTRONGEST EVIDENCE: cold state, CI xdist config, no pre-step -> 63 passed in 442.35s with NO worker\nkill. The identical cold state on the old code killed two workers at the 300s cliff (2 failed, 12\npassed, 7 skipped in 306.60s). 442 seconds of provisioning, none of it charged to a test.\n\n2601 also picked up a Verified -1, confirmed to be the same 9a17 signature (gw0 crash on\ntest_emitted_bpmn_has_no_illegal_ids; 1 failed, 19165 passed). Posted the evidence to Gerrit and did\nNOT recheck. Both 2594 and 2601 are now blocked purely on 2600 landing -- one defect gating three\nchanges.\n\nFiled 6f66-41ff-cfc0-403b / undesirous-undiseased-zebu (priority 3) for an order-dependent failure in\ntest_anthropic_lazy_import_isolation_heldout, surfaced incidentally: passes standalone, failed once in\na full run under random ordering, with tests/e2e not even collected. Filed as a CANDIDATE on a single\nobservation -- AC1 deliberately requires a deterministic reproduction BEFORE any fix, and AC2 requires\nestablishing the direction of the leak, because 'polluted by an earlier test' and 'insufficiently\nisolated itself' have different fixes and guessing between them is how a flake gets papered over. A\nheld-out ISOLATION oracle that is itself order-sensitive is a bad case: its purpose is to prove\nisolation, so an isolation failure makes the signal ambiguous exactly where it must be sharp."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788493481850672001,"uuid":"e3a7f1b1-52ca-404a-9b7b-857662c4c342"}